{
 "id": "2016-wyles-coastal",
 "name": "Coastal Litter and Restoration – Scene Ratings",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Study 3 of Wyles, Pahl, Thomas & Thompson (2016), Environment and Behavior 48(9): 1095–1126. Participants imagine sitting on a rocky British shore and see four versions of the same view in random order: clean, drift seaweed, fishing litter and public litter (the paper's Figure 1 photographs, CC BY 3.0). For each view they rate how attractive it is and how willing they would be to stay in a hotel with that view (preference), how the scene makes them feel (very sad to very happy; very calm to very excited) and whether they could rest and recover there (restoration likelihood), all on 1–10 scales. The four views are then shown again with their ratings and participants write why they responded that way, followed by the 4-item short Connectedness to Nature Scale (1–5). Ratings and explanations are keyed by condition for a one-way within-subject comparison. In the paper, clean and seaweed scenes were rated above the midpoint and littered scenes below, with public litter rated lowest; arousal did not differ. Differences from the original study: (1) all four conditions use the one backdrop printed in Figure 1, whereas Study 3 drew each condition from four different backdrops; (2) the images are cropped from the published figure and are smaller than the originals; (3) runs online rather than in the lab, without the practice ratings on four extra images; (4) age and gender are asked at the start.",
